UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 33, Lesson 1: Folktales from around the world', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Folktales from around the world often share patterns such as tricksters, journeys or rewards; readers study published versions and respect cultural sources named in the book.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same strategy to an unfamiliar topic or audience, then compare the effect of two different choices.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nFrom a published folktale you know, name one pattern (trick, journey, reward) and the source book if possible.\n\nFEEDBACK\nAnswer should cite a real published children''s version available to the student, not an invented tradition.\n\nCHECK\nAssuming every culture shares the same moral sticker erases difference.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nCompare two published folktales from different places (classroom/library). Chart pattern, setting clues, problem and ending. Record each book''s title and any culture/source note in the book.\n\nWrite a short reflection describing one specific revision you made and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"English","learning_intention":"Folktales from around the world often share patterns such as tricksters, journeys or rewards; readers study published versions and respect cultural sources named in the book.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Reading","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["EN2-RECOM-01","EN2-UARL-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Compare two published folktales from different places (classroom/library). Chart pattern, setting clues, problem and ending. Record each book''s title and any culture/source note in the book. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: Answer should cite a real published children''s version available to the student, not an invented tradition. Reasoning: I used folktale by attending to pattern and explain why it matters rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Assuming every culture shares the same moral sticker erases difference. Correction: Answer should cite a real published children''s version available to the student, not an invented tradition. Why: the correction restores accurate use of folktale.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Assuming every culture shares the same moral sticker erases difference. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating folktale on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with folktale, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail. / Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Answer should cite a real published children''s version available to the student, not an invented tradition. This matches the lesson skill for folktale.","options":["Copy the worked example and call it independent work.","Answer should cite a real published children''s version available to the student, not an invented tradition.","Assuming every culture shares the same moral sticker erases difference.","Ignore the task and invent an unrelated answer."],"question":"From a published folktale you know, name one pattern (trick, journey, reward) and the source book if possible. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Assuming every culture shares the same moral sticker erases difference. That misconception weakens accuracy; the other choices align with the lesson idea.","options":["Answer should cite a real published children''s version available to the student, not an invented tradition.","Careful use of pattern and explain why it matters with clear evidence.","Assuming every culture shares the same moral sticker erases difference.","Folktales from around the world often share patterns such as tricksters, journeys or rewards; readers study published versions and respect cultural sources…"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when practising folktale?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and named pattern and explain why it matters, not by copying the model.","options":["Apply the idea with new evidence and name the pattern and explain why it matters used.","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence.","Choose randomly because any option could be right.","Treat a missing detail as a proven fact."],"question":"A learner is practising folktale. Which approach best shows understanding?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The skill is shown by quoting or pointing to wording that carries folktale.","options":["Point to exact words in the sentence that show folktale and say what they do.","Ignore the sentence and invent a more exciting event.","Say the writing is good without naming any words.","Assuming every culture shares the same moral sticker erases difference."],"question":"Read: \"After the blackout, Tess lit a candle and read the crumpled note.\" Which choice best uses folktale from this lesson?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"The correct statement applies the language skill; the others miss the evidence.","options":["Assuming every culture shares the same moral sticker erases difference.","Ignore the task and invent an unrelated answer.","Copy the worked example and call it independent work.","Answer should cite a real published children''s version available to the student, not an invented tradition."],"question":"Which statement correctly applies the lesson idea about folktale?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Start from a specific textual clue, then explain its effect.","options":["Copy a model sentence from another story unchanged.","Treat a missing detail as a proven fact.","Locate a specific word, phrase or structural clue before deciding.","Guess from the title alone with no textual clue."],"question":"In a new text, what should you do first to practise pattern and explain why it matters?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Repair means fixing meaning or evidence, not padding.","options":["Change the inaccurate idea or missing evidence, not only the length.","Add adjectives without fixing the wrong idea.","Make the answer longer while keeping the same mistake.","Delete the evidence that disagreed with your first guess."],"question":"Which revision actually repairs meaning?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Transfer needs fresh material with an explained language choice.","options":["Change fonts and claim the work is new.","Assuming every culture shares the same moral sticker erases difference.","Use a fresh sentence or text and explain one precise choice about folktale.","Retype the worked model as the final answer."],"question":"Which response shows transfer of folktale to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guid