y option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore the example.","Prefer evidence over wording that merely sounds clever."],"submit":["Check that your response and reason are both present.","Proofread names, sequence and punctuation.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact word or action before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and underline one useful detail.","Say what that detail helps a reader understand.","A vague compliment is weaker than quoted evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak attempt with a stronger revision.","Change meaning or accuracy, not only length.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh topic that still uses the same skill.","Explain one decision in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include an original response and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new content."]},"subject":"English","success_criteria":["I can use folktale with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify pattern and explain why it matters.","I can practise culture independently on a new prompt.","I can notice published needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Folktales from around the world often share patterns such as tricksters, journeys or rewards; readers study published versions and respect cultural sources named in the book. A supported explanation follows the actual words and purpose of the task. Apply the lesson idea with…","options":["Folktales from around the world often share patterns such as tricksters, journeys or rewards; readers study published versions and respect cultural sources named in the book.","A claim unrelated to the text.","An answer copied without understandin